  "notes": "new rig: NI USB-6453 (one card), coarse approach by screws (actuator none), piezo driver in STANDARD mode, current amplifier with TWO outputs. Wiring: piezo command ao2, bias ao1, junction voltage ai1, current channel 1 ai2, current channel 2 ai4, piezo sense ai3; all inputs RSE (BNC centre to the AI+ terminal, shield to AI GND or nowhere). Channel 1 (ai_current) must be the LOWER-gain output, the one that follows the junction through contact: contact is judged on it. Channel 2 (ai_current_2) is the higher-gain output: the junction-open test (the Break threshold) is judged on it, and it is recorded and drawn beside channel 1. PLACEHOLDERS to replace with this rig's own numbers: ai_current_2 (which input channel 2 is on), cal.preamp_gain_v_per_a and cal.preamp_gain_2_v_per_a (the two gains, V/A; 1e4 and 1e7 here), cal.piezo_nm_per_volt (standard mode: the stage's range in nm divided by 10 V) and cal.sense_nm_per_volt (the same number in standard mode; refit with rigtests/01_piezo_sweep.py), keithley.series_resistance_ohm (the resistor between the bias output and the junction; 0 if there is none). sim.ai_noise_v_rms is for --simulate only: 88 uV rms is NI's idle-channel noise for the USB-6453 on its +/-10 V range, so a simulated run shows the noise floors the card will give."
